Physical Medium can be Copper (electrical), Fibre (light) or WIFI (radio frequencies RF)

Layer 1 (Physical) specifics the transmission and reception of RAW BIT STREAMS between a device and a SHARED physical medium. It defines things like voltage levels, timing, rates, distance, modulation and connectors. 

Layer 1 Device will only understand Layer 1 but a Layer 3 Device will only understand Layer 1, 2, 3. 

## Layer 1 (HUB):

Lets say you want more then 2 Players, How will that be possible?

SO, you will have to use a HUB. So all laptops will need to connect to the HUB.

Remember, anything the HUB receives on any ports, is transmitted to the other ports like:

- Errors & Collisions

### Layer 1 Networking Key info:

- No device addressing, all data is processed by all devices
- One Laptop cannot address one singular laptop it is a broadcast medium
- This is a limitation of Layer 1 but it is fixed on Layer 2
- If multiple devices try to transmit at once a collision will occur
- Layer 1 has no media access control (can’t control what devices transmit) and no collision detection
- Layer 1 can’t detect when collision occur]
- Layer 2 runs on a working layer 1
